1
00:00:00,258 --> 00:00:03,662
Radial Angioplasty: Heart
Stenting Through the Wrist,

2
00:00:03,722 --> 00:00:08,293
explained by Dr. Mohd Maqbool
Sohil, Interventional Cardiologist.

3
00:00:08,817 --> 00:00:11,327
A build-up of plaque can narrow a coronary

4
00:00:11,377 --> 00:00:14,865
artery and reduce blood
flow to the heart muscle.

5
00:00:15,422 --> 00:00:20,339
This can cause angina or, if a clot
blocks the artery, a heart attack.

6
00:00:20,658 --> 00:00:22,979
You stay awake on the X-ray table.

7
00:00:23,596 --> 00:00:27,239
A local anaesthetic numbs the
skin over the radial artery,

8
00:00:27,334 --> 00:00:29,203
on the thumb side of the wrist.

9
00:00:30,729 --> 00:00:35,324
A thin needle enters the artery,
a fine wire is passed through it,

10
00:00:35,384 --> 00:00:39,670
and a small tube, called a
sheath, slides in over the wire.

11
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:42,024
No cut, no stitches.

12
00:00:42,458 --> 00:00:46,386
Medicines are given through the
sheath: heparin to prevent clotting,

13
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:49,910
and a vasodilator to prevent
spasm of the wrist artery.

14
00:00:50,358 --> 00:00:55,284
Under X-ray guidance, a guidewire
and a thin tube, called a catheter,

15
00:00:55,334 --> 00:01:00,334
travel up the arm arteries to the aorta
and the opening of the coronary artery.

16
00:01:01,318 --> 00:01:05,413
Contrast, an X-ray dye, is
injected through the catheter.

17
00:01:06,030 --> 00:01:11,339
The X-ray pictures, called an angiogram,
show where the artery is narrowed.

18
00:01:11,762 --> 00:01:15,191
A very thin guidewire is
passed across the narrowing.

19
00:01:15,855 --> 00:01:20,117
A balloon is inflated there to press
the plaque into the artery wall.

20
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:25,581
A drug-eluting stent, a fine
metal mesh coated with medicine,

21
00:01:25,631 --> 00:01:27,572
is expanded by its balloon.

22
00:01:28,141 --> 00:01:32,177
The balloon is removed; the stent
stays to keep the artery open.

23
00:01:32,604 --> 00:01:36,566
The sheath is removed and a compression
band is placed on the wrist,

24
00:01:36,616 --> 00:01:37,949
with no stitches.

25
00:01:38,519 --> 00:01:41,816
The band is loosened
gradually over a few hours.

26
00:01:43,624 --> 00:01:46,991
You can usually sit up
and walk soon afterwards,

27
00:01:47,041 --> 00:01:50,981
without the long bed rest often
needed after groin access.

28
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:55,003
Usually home the next day;
individual cases vary.

29
00:01:55,404 --> 00:01:58,533
Compared with the groin,
or femoral, route,

30
00:01:58,583 --> 00:02:02,571
the wrist route has fewer bleeding
and access-site complications.

31
00:02:03,128 --> 00:02:06,938
ESC guidelines recommend it
as the standard approach.

32
00:02:07,424 --> 00:02:10,520
As with any procedure, there are risks.

33
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,934
Your cardiologist will discuss
whether the wrist route suits you;

34
00:02:15,005 --> 00:02:18,172
the groin is used when the
wrist is not suitable.

35
00:02:18,484 --> 00:02:19,389
Key points.

36
00:02:20,065 --> 00:02:22,720
A needle puncture under local anaesthetic,

37
00:02:22,863 --> 00:02:26,172
with no cut, no stitches,
and you stay awake.

38
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:29,303
A catheter goes from
the wrist to the heart,

39
00:02:29,422 --> 00:02:33,803
a balloon widens the narrowing, and
a drug-eluting stent keeps it open.

40
00:02:34,399 --> 00:02:38,291
Afterwards, a wrist band is
loosened over a few hours;

41
00:02:38,399 --> 00:02:42,660
you can sit up and walk soon, and
usually go home the next day.

42
00:02:43,058 --> 00:02:48,236
This animation is for education and is
not a substitute for medical advice.

